# DraggableElement

`DraggableElement` is the low-level drag primitive of the cropper. It renders a `<div>`, follows a single mouse pointer or finger that starts on it, and reports how far the pointer moved since the previous event. Every interactive part of a stencil is built on it: the stencil's move area ([`DraggableArea`](/docs/components/DraggableArea), the same component under another name), and the wrappers inside every [handler](/docs/components/SimpleHandler) and [line](/docs/components/SimpleLine).

## Usage

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { DraggableElement, type MoveDirections } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let position = $state({ left: 0, top: 0 });

	function onMove({ left, top }: MoveDirections) {
		position = { left: position.left + left, top: position.top + top };
	}
</script>

<DraggableElement {onMove} class="knob">
	<div style="translate: {position.left}px {position.top}px">Drag me</div>
</DraggableElement>
```

## Props

| Prop                 | Type                                                                       | Default | Description                                                                                                                                       |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `onMove`             | <code>(directions: MoveDirections, nativeEvent: MouseEvent &#124; TouchEvent) =&gt; void</code> | —       | Called for each pointer move during a drag, with the `{ left, top }` offset in pixels since the previous call.                                   |
| `onMoveStart`        | `() => void`                                                               | —       | Called when a drag starts (on `mousedown` with the left button, or `touchstart` with one finger).                                                |
| `onMoveEnd`          | `() => void`                                                               | —       | Called when the drag ends.                                                                                                                        |
| `onEnter`            | `() => void`                                                               | —       | Called when the pointer enters the element or a touch starts on it. Handlers and lines use it for their hover state.                            |
| `onLeave`            | `() => void`                                                               | —       | Called when the pointer leaves, or when a drag ends. During a drag, leaving the element doesn't count.                                         |
| `disabled`           | `boolean`                                                                  | `false` | Ignores input. Disabling it mid-drag cancels the drag.                                                                                            |
| `useAnchor`          | `boolean`                                                                  | `true`  | Suppresses movement on an axis while the pointer travels back toward the point where it grabbed the element. See below.                          |
| `activationDistance` | `number`                                                                   | `30`    | For touch, how many pixels the finger must travel before the drag starts, so that a tap or a slightly shaky finger doesn't move anything. Handlers and lines use `0`. |
| `class`              | `ClassValue`                                                               | —       | Classes for the element. Upstream's `className`.                                                                                                  |
| `children`           | `Snippet`                                                                  | —       | The content.                                                                                                                                      |

### The anchor

When the user drags a stencil into a limit (the edge of the image, say) and keeps going, the stencil stays put while the pointer moves on. Without help, moving the pointer back would immediately pull the stencil away from the limit, so it would no longer sit under the pointer. With `useAnchor`, movement on an axis is ignored while the pointer heads back toward the spot where it originally grabbed the element, and resumes once it passes it. The element then stays under the same point of the pointer.

## Event handling

The element listens with native, non-passive listeners: `mousedown` and `touchstart` on itself, and `mousemove`, `mouseup`, `touchmove` and `touchend` on `window`. It stops the propagation of the events it handles, and prevents their default action during a drag. That is why dragging the stencil doesn't also move the image underneath, and why a drag doesn't scroll the page on touch screens.

<Admonition type="info" title="Why not on* attributes">

Svelte delegates `onmousedown` to the application root and makes `ontouchstart` passive. Neither can stop the image's listener in time or prevent scrolling, so the component registers its listeners with `on()` from `svelte/events` inside an attachment. Keep this in mind if you write your own draggable parts.

</Admonition>

## CSS classes

| Element | Classes                                                   |
| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| Root    | `advanced-cropper-draggable-element`, plus your `class`   |

The base class has no styles. It exists as a hook for your CSS and for themes.
